155
00:14:37,660 --> 00:14:42,780
Psychology research has tended to portray solitude as a negative experience.

156
00:14:43,960 --> 00:14:50,100
Studies conducted in the 1970s and 1990s suggested that people felt less happy when

157
00:14:50,100 --> 00:14:52,640
alone as compared to being with others.

158
00:14:53,480 --> 00:14:59,420
However, a new paper shows an alternative view of solitude, one in which solitude

159
00:14:59,420 --> 00:15:00,420
can be positive.

160
00:15:01,140 --> 00:15:03,660
Let's start by looking at the earlier research.

161
00:15:03,660 --> 00:15:06,420
It had a couple of shortcomings.

162
00:15:07,680 --> 00:15:11,340
First, it measured emotion on a scale from positive to negative,

163
00:15:12,040 --> 00:15:16,440
overlooking the possibility that our positive and negative emotions can fluctuate

164
00:15:16,440 --> 00:15:22,540
independently. Also, it categorized emotions as simply positive or negative.

165
00:15:23,520 --> 00:15:28,100
It didn't consider that emotions arouse us to different degrees, and that both

166
00:15:28,100 --> 00:15:31,800
positive and negative emotions can arouse us a lot or a little.

167
00:15:31,800 --> 00:15:37,560
is, whether positive or negative, emotions can be either high arousal or low

168
00:15:37,560 --> 00:15:43,920
arousal. High arousal emotions include excitement on the positive side or anger on

169
00:15:43,920 --> 00:15:49,400
the negative side, while low arousal ones include feeling calm on the positive side

170
00:15:49,400 --> 00:15:51,420
or lonely on the negative.

171
00:15:52,060 --> 00:15:55,280
This new research attempted to overcome these shortcomings.

172
00:15:56,080 --> 00:15:58,160
Researchers began with a simple study.

173
00:15:58,160 --> 00:16:03,440
They asked participants to spend 15 minutes sitting alone without engaging in any

174
00:16:03,440 --> 00:16:07,860
activity and measured how this solitude influenced their emotional state.

175
00:16:08,360 --> 00:16:13,280
This experiment specifically aimed to determine the effect of solitude on high

176
00:16:13,280 --> 00:16:18,960
arousal emotions. It looked at positive emotions, such as being excited or

177
00:16:18,960 --> 00:16:24,100
interested, and negative emotions, including being scared or distressed.

178
00:16:24,740 --> 00:16:26,260
The results were clear.

179
00:16:26,260 --> 00:16:31,860
After 15 minutes of solitude, the participants showed reductions in both types of

180
00:16:31,860 --> 00:16:37,060
emotion. A second study measured the effects of solitude on low arousal emotions.

181
00:16:37,860 --> 00:16:43,240
These included both positive and negative emotions, such as feeling calm, relaxed,

182
00:16:43,480 --> 00:16:44,740
sad, or lonely.

183
00:16:45,260 --> 00:16:49,640
That experiment found that all of these emotions were increased by time alone.

184
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:53,660
Thus, it seems past depictions of solitude were wrong.

185
00:16:53,660 --> 00:16:58,760
It doesn't have a simple emotional effect that can be characterized as good or bad.

186
00:16:59,520 --> 00:17:02,680
Rather, it changes the intensity of our inner experience.

187
00:17:03,320 --> 00:17:09,020
It amplifies quieter emotions, but it diminishes the intensity of stronger feelings.

188
00:17:09,680 --> 00:17:14,020
It's worth clarifying that these findings relate to relatively brief periods of

189
00:17:14,020 --> 00:17:17,680
solitude. This is distinct from prolonged loneliness.

190
00:17:17,680 --> 00:17:22,380
Research has demonstrated that the latter is correlated with an assortment of

191
00:17:22,380 --> 00:17:24,980
negative physical and psychological effects.

192
00:17:25,720 --> 00:17:27,600
How can people benefit from being alone?

193
00:17:28,120 --> 00:17:32,680
The findings here suggest that people can use solitude to regulate their emotions.

194
00:17:33,400 --> 00:17:38,840
Solitude can help us become quiet after excitement, calm after an angry episode, or

195
00:17:38,840 --> 00:17:41,080
simply feel at peace.

